// std/runtime/wait: the waits on a descriptor, in machine words.
//
// What `std/io` builds its waits on, and what the VM calls to serve the waits of
// the program it runs. The runtime serves these, and under the VM the host serves
// them with its own.
//
// Apart from `std/runtime/scheduler`: a program that loads the scheduler puts a
// gate on every `@singleton` and every constant whose value runs code, and almost
// every program loads `std/io` (ADR 0129). This module imports nothing.

module "std/runtime/wait"

/// Park the running thread until descriptor `fd` is as `readiness` says, while
/// the other threads run: input to read or its end, or room for a write or a
/// failure. With no other thread, it returns `true` at once, and the read or
/// write that follows waits on the system thread.
///
/// `blocked` says that a read or write of `fd` just found it would block: the
/// wait may then park without asking `fd` first, and trust what the system
/// reports when it wakes. Called before any read, it could park on input that
/// already arrived. With no other thread, a blocked wait waits on the system
/// thread itself; on Windows, a descriptor other than a socket returns at
/// once, and the read waits.
///
/// @param fd         a descriptor of the process. On Windows, a read waits on
///                   0 for stdin, else the read end of a pipe
///                   `create_watched_pipe` made, as its address. A blocked wait
///                   takes one set not to block, whose last read or write
///                   failed with `EAGAIN`.
/// @param readiness  what to wait for.
/// @param nanos      the longest wait; a negative one waits with no deadline.
/// @param blocked    whether the last read or write of `fd` found it would
///                   block.
/// @return           `false` once `nanos` have passed with `fd` still not
///                   ready.
export wait_for :: fn(fd: i64, readiness: Readiness, nanos: i64, blocked: bool) -> bool =
    Intrinsic.wait_for(fd, readiness, nanos, blocked)

/// End the waits of the threads parked on descriptor `fd`: each returns as if
/// `fd` were ready, and asks what it waited for again. Called before `fd` is
/// closed, since the system hands its number out again.
///
/// @param fd  a descriptor of the process, about to be closed.
export stop_waiting :: fn(fd: i64) -> void = Intrinsic.stop_waiting(fd)
